WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.134
-(APPLAUSE)
-Okay, welcome to Overtime.

00:00:02.134 --> 00:00:03.068
We have Professor of Marketing

00:00:03.135 --> 00:00:05.638
at NYU Stern School of Business,
Scott Galloway.

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:08.674
Former Governor Andrew Cuomo's
top aide Melissa DeRosa.

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:11.110
Former governor of New York,
Andrew Cuomo.

00:00:11.176 --> 00:00:14.113
-Okay, first question is...
-(APPLAUSE)

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.148
...for you.

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:19.585
"Is Trump right to think
that the cases that Alvin Bragg

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:23.056
and Tish James brought
against him are politically--"

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:25.057
-Okay. Well, there's a reversal.
-(MELISSA DEROSA LAUGHS)

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.293
Right. Because the same people--
Right.

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:32.599
Alvin Bragg, that's the one
about the hush money

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.200
-to Stormy Daniels, right?
-MELISSA: Right.

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.036
And Tish James is the one
about his business dealings.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:38.071
Apparently, he's a crook.

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.940
(LAUGHTER)

00:00:41.006 --> 00:00:42.075
"Is Trump right to think

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:43.877
those cases
are politically motivated?"

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:46.212
The, uh...

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:48.715
First, Trump is never right,
in my book. Just to--

00:00:48.781 --> 00:00:53.720
-Just to start this off.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:55.955
BILL MAHER: Was there ever
a time when you were friends?

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.224
I mean, he goes way back
in New York as you do.

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.225
He goes way back.

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:00.660
We're both from Queens,
New York.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:02.528
-Right, that's what I mean.
-Our families knew each other.

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:05.498
Uh, I dealt with him extensively
through COVID,

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:07.133 line:0%
literally on a daily basis.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.368 line:0%
Uh. No, we were never friends.

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:11.638 line:0%
-(LAUGHTER)
-BILL: Okay.

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:15.641
The-- What is interesting
about the question is the--

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:16.809
There was actually a poll.

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:18.811
Alvin Bragg,
the Manhattan district attorney

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.747
brought the first case
against Trump.

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:23.216
This is before Mar-a-Lago,
before January 6th,

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:27.153
which were, in my opinion,
frankly more serious cases.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.655
But, they did a poll

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.957
after Alvin Bragg
brought the case.

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:35.461
Seventy-six percent
of Americans said

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:37.096
it was politically influenced.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:38.397
Seventy-six percent.

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:40.366
Seventy-six percent of Americans

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.702
don't agree on the color
of the flag, right?

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:46.706
But 76 percent said
it was politically influenced.

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:48.975
And I think that
is telling, Bill,

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:53.279
because if-- if, uh,
we don't trust government,

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:54.647
we don't trust the media,
et cetera.

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:59.785
If this country believes
that the justice system itself

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:02.789
is then politically tainted,
then we have real trouble.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:05.057
'Cause then you're on
the precipice of anarchy.

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:07.393
But that's what
I found telling about that.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.530
It was more the public opinion
was so high

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:13.565
-that politics...
-BILL: Yeah.

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:14.833
...is influencing
the justice system.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.635
And, I mean,
Trump is the kind of guy,

00:02:16.702 --> 00:02:18.938
the more he goes to jail,
the more popular he becomes.

00:02:19.004 --> 00:02:21.774
It's like-- It's street cred.
He's like a rapper.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.142
(LAUGHTER)

00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:24.210
-BILL: But it's true.
-It's true.

00:02:24.276 --> 00:02:25.210
It's absolutely true.

00:02:25.477 --> 00:02:28.314
And also, I never thought
they should have brought the--

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:30.416
the Stormy Daniels one.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.685
And the fact that there's four.
One, it just--

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:36.022
To the people
who don't follow it closely,

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:37.656
you know,
they should have just had one--

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:40.325
one "he's generally
a piece of shit" trial.

00:02:40.392 --> 00:02:41.527
(LAUGHTER)

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:44.697
But all--
One omnibus piece of shit...

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.866
-GPOS case. Yeah.
-Yeah. (LAUGHS)

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:50.569
MELISSA:
And the Stormy Daniels case...

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.372
The Stormy Daniels case,
it's like you're asking America

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:55.207
to then think of
this former porn star

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:56.876 line:0%
-as a hero, and Michael Cohen...
-BILL: Right.

00:02:56.942 --> 00:02:58.978 line:0%
...who's a known liar and fixer,
as a hero,

00:02:59.044 --> 00:03:00.780 line:0%
and it was really
a books and records case

00:03:00.846 --> 00:03:03.249 line:0%
that was up-charged to beat
the statute of limitations.

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:05.618 line:0%
So, yeah, it made it really easy
to roll your eyes

00:03:05.684 --> 00:03:07.153 line:0%
on the other ones
that were more serious.

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:11.357
The fear is that hush money,
or inflating your assets,

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:14.493
it does reduce the veracity
of much more serious crimes.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.396
-BILL: Right.
-Including nuclear secrets,

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:18.797
and trying to overturn
the peaceful transfer of power.

00:03:18.964 --> 00:03:21.768
I think it was just a tactical
error to go after the first two.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.002
-BILL: Okay.
-ANDREW CUOMO: Here, here.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.104
Melissa, "Is there a way
to make politics

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:27.105
less about personal vendettas,

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:29.308
and more about governing?"

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:30.943
Yeah, again that's why
I said at the beginning,

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.213
I see why you two were pissed,
because like, I mean,

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.716
Democrats at their best,
I think, are wonks.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:38.718
And I mean that as
a great compliment.

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:40.552
It's the one thing
that Republicans, the--

00:03:40.652 --> 00:03:43.122
You don't usually find
Republican wonks.

00:03:43.188 --> 00:03:45.157
They're Reagan.
"Give me it on one page."

00:03:45.357 --> 00:03:46.926
Bush.
"Give it to me on one page."

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:48.560
You know, they're one-pagers.

00:03:49.161 --> 00:03:51.063
And that's--
Government is complicated.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.299
And I know you two
put in the time.

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:55.334
Government is complicated,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.603
and sometimes
it requires sharp elbows,

00:03:57.703 --> 00:04:00.506
and sometimes it requires you
to have to be tough

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:02.207
in order to be effective.

00:04:02.474 --> 00:04:04.110
But the thing I find
so funny about it is

00:04:04.176 --> 00:04:06.845
in the reaction to my book,
some of it is people saying,

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:08.647
"Oh, this is--
You know, she wants to go out

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.048
and attack people
that attacked them."

00:04:10.115 --> 00:04:11.750
And I'm like,
"Okay, well, there was a point

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:13.819
when you were comparing me
to Hitler's enabler,

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:15.521
but now we're like,
'Whoa, whoa, whoa,

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.824
don't criticize anybody. Don't,
you know, don't call this out.'"

00:04:18.957 --> 00:04:20.292
And really,
what I find interesting,

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:22.495
is nobody wants to talk about
the facts of what happened,

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:23.963
'cause when you look at
the facts of what happened,

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.030
it's so damning.

00:04:25.097 --> 00:04:27.133
So it's easy to make it
about personalities

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.734
and about vendettas,
and about everything else.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.437
Well, I feel like
those things carry the day.

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:33.839
To answer that question
I asked you at the beginning.

00:04:33.973 --> 00:04:35.975
You know,
"Why would a liberal state

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:38.811
with a liberal paper
go after a liberal governor?"

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:41.647
Because, I'm sorry to make it
all about generations,

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:43.749
but a lot of
the younger generation

00:04:43.815 --> 00:04:45.151
who was raised on the phone,

00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:48.121
which makes you shady
and passive aggressive

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:49.621
and mean and fake.

00:04:49.721 --> 00:04:50.956
I'm sorry, it just does.

00:04:51.023 --> 00:04:52.091
-(LAUGHTER)
-I don't-- I don't think--

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.493
You think they care
about policy first and foremost?

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.129
I don't. I think they take glee
in this kind of shit.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:58.130
They like taking--

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:00.333
Especially that nest of vipers
that's Albany.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.102
Yeah. No, they love it.
It's sport. It's actual sport.

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:05.371
It's sport. It's always like,
"He's on my same team."

00:05:05.504 --> 00:05:07.874
That's...
Who gives a shit whose team--

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:09.408
You know what? I'm on my team.

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:11.310
-And it's, you know, so-- so--
-(APPLAUSE)

00:05:11.376 --> 00:05:13.379
Now, again,
I don't know what happened,

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:18.951
and I don't know who's at fault,
but I know there's two sides.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:21.620
Okay, Scott,
"How can we prevent China

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:26.392
from using AI to sow chaos
and disrupt our elections?"

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:28.294
Well, we can't.

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:32.464
(LAUGHTER)

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:33.732
It's too late.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:37.603 line:0%
You're about to see an
AI misinformation lollapalooza

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:41.641 line:0%
in Q1 and Q2 as Putin recognizes
the fastest blue line path

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:44.543 line:0%
between him and victory
in Ukraine

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.278 line:0%
is the re-election
of Donald Trump.

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:49.749 line:0%
So if you're spending 70 billion
and 100,000 lives a year

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.884
fighting a losing war
in Ukraine,

00:05:51.951 --> 00:05:54.821
wouldn't you be stupid
not to spend five or 10 billion

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:58.457
on Albanian troll farms,
A/B tested information,

00:05:58.724 --> 00:06:01.159
and then an amoral
management team in social media

00:06:01.226 --> 00:06:03.262
who will feel really bad
about the misinformation

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:04.497
the day after the election

00:06:04.563 --> 00:06:06.031
when all the checks
have cleared?

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:09.902
We are about to see the
weaponization of media platforms

00:06:10.102 --> 00:06:12.971
that have no fidelity
to the commonwealth,

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:19.378
and the GRU and AI
de-position Biden and Harris.

00:06:19.511 --> 00:06:21.947
We're going to see
the first real externality

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:25.685
of mis-- of AI
in Q1 and Q2 of next year,

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:27.486
in terms of misinformation
around the election.

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:29.489
But Ozempic's
still more important?

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:32.725
-(LAUGHTER, APPLAUSE)
-I-- I-- I--

00:06:32.792 --> 00:06:35.127
I still don't have the answer
to that question.

00:06:35.193 --> 00:06:36.095
Okay.

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:39.264
Governor Cuomo,
"Knowing what you know now,

00:06:39.431 --> 00:06:40.633
is there anything
you would change

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:42.234
about the way
you would administer--

00:06:42.401 --> 00:06:43.468
administration handled COVID?"

00:06:43.535 --> 00:06:45.337
Okay, that's the question
we didn't get to,

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:48.641
and I was gonna read you
the ProPublica statement.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:52.211
Which-- They basically said
New York was the only state--

00:06:52.344 --> 00:06:53.578
Now, of course, the scandal,

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:55.848
the issue involved,
if people don't remember,

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:57.015
is that, you know,

00:06:57.082 --> 00:06:59.986
obviously this is
the beginning of the pandemic,

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:01.053
everything was chaotic,

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.288
we didn't know
what was going on.

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:05.691
But, uh, we did know this,

00:07:05.791 --> 00:07:08.127
it mostly affected
the elderly people,

00:07:08.193 --> 00:07:09.528
like most pathogens do.

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:10.763
They were the most vulnerable.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.966
So, you allowed people
who had been in the hospital,

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.434
older people
from a nursing home,

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.735
now they go to the hospital,

00:07:16.835 --> 00:07:19.038
to go back
into the nursing home

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:21.240
without testing them.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.443
That pub-- ProPublica says
you were the only state to do it

00:07:24.543 --> 00:07:25.544
without testing them

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.312
when they went back
into the nursing home.

00:07:27.446 --> 00:07:29.248
And that's what caused
such death in the nursing homes.

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:30.182
-Yeah.
-Is that true?

00:07:30.315 --> 00:07:32.918
No. Short answer is no.

00:07:33.252 --> 00:07:35.755
Also, remember,
you say what we knew.

00:07:37.189 --> 00:07:39.258 line:0%
First, this
is Monday morning quarterbacking

00:07:39.324 --> 00:07:41.393 line:0%
by which I could make
the New York Jets champion,

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:42.561 line:0%
right, if we're gonna do this.

00:07:42.794 --> 00:07:46.966 line:0%
The--
When COVID started, it was--

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:49.735 line:0%
All the disinformation
was amazing, right?

00:07:49.901 --> 00:07:53.272 line:0%
It was coming from China,
wet market, zootrophic virus.

00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:56.742
It was going to California
and the state of Washington.

00:07:56.842 --> 00:08:01.280
So we banned travel
to-- to China-- from China.

00:08:01.614 --> 00:08:05.918
It turned out that China
had already spread it to Europe.

00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:09.622
All the European flights
were coming to New York, JFK.

00:08:10.256 --> 00:08:13.825
So, it had been here for months,
and it was astronomical.

00:08:14.426 --> 00:08:16.228
When we first found out
about it,

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:18.497
and Scott can appreciate this,

00:08:18.897 --> 00:08:22.802
they were projecting we would
need 150,000 hospital beds

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:24.903
to deal with the number
of infected.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:26.772
-I remember.
-We only had 50,000

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:29.007
in the entire state of New York.

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:30.842
-So we were--
-BILL: So you did it
to free up beds?

00:08:30.976 --> 00:08:33.979
We were afraid
of losing hospital beds,

00:08:34.613 --> 00:08:37.850
but people who were
in hospitals,

00:08:37.983 --> 00:08:40.152
who were considered
medically stable,

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:41.587
who were tested,

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.489
were sent to nursing homes

00:08:43.622 --> 00:08:48.594
if the nursing home said
they could treat that person

00:08:48.828 --> 00:08:50.562
in a way that protects
the other people

00:08:50.662 --> 00:08:51.697
in the nursing home.

00:08:51.931 --> 00:08:53.031
And that was a way to make sure

00:08:53.098 --> 00:08:54.633
-we had enough hospital--
-But it didn't, did it?

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.635
I mean, they also--

00:08:56.769 --> 00:08:59.005
I mean, the same organization,
ProPublica,

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:02.074
says you counted the number
who died differently.

00:09:02.207 --> 00:09:03.742
In other words,
you only counted the ones

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.444
who died in the nursing home.

00:09:05.577 --> 00:09:07.613
Whereas many people came
from their nursing home

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.649
and died in the hospital,
and you didn't count those.

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:11.750
You only counted if they died
in the nursing home,

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:13.385
which they felt was deliberate.

00:09:13.485 --> 00:09:15.554
Well, let me now get
weeds-y with you.

00:09:15.821 --> 00:09:17.990
We got--
Every day I did a briefing.

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:20.359
And every day
we collected information

00:09:20.425 --> 00:09:22.427
from the hospitals
and from nursing homes.

00:09:22.761 --> 00:09:25.063
And we printed two numbers
on the screen every day.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:27.065
This is the number of people
who died in a hospital,

00:09:27.165 --> 00:09:29.034
this is the number of people
who died in a nursing home.

00:09:29.368 --> 00:09:33.005
Which means you had to go back
every day to 1,000 hospitals,

00:09:33.105 --> 00:09:34.806
nursing homes,
to collect this data.

00:09:35.574 --> 00:09:37.042
And this how many died
in a hospital,

00:09:37.108 --> 00:09:38.610
this is how many died
in a nursing home.

00:09:39.044 --> 00:09:40.779
They then raised the question
and said, well,

00:09:41.012 --> 00:09:43.749
"How many died in a hospital

00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:47.786
who were originally
in a nursing home

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.956
before they went
to the hospital?"

00:09:51.223 --> 00:09:52.524
That's a different question.

00:09:52.991 --> 00:09:57.896
And that actually took
the state controller a year

00:09:57.963 --> 00:10:00.299
to figure out and go back
and do the audit.

00:10:00.899 --> 00:10:02.434
But, we said
this is how many died

00:10:02.501 --> 00:10:03.535
in a nursing home,

00:10:03.602 --> 00:10:05.037
this is how many died
in a hospital.

00:10:05.471 --> 00:10:06.572
Different question.

00:10:06.705 --> 00:10:08.740
"How many died in hospitals

00:10:08.974 --> 00:10:11.210
who were originally
in nursing homes?"

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:13.412
You then have to
go back and forensically--

00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:14.713
So you wouldn't do
anything differently?

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.615
-I mean, is it fair just--
-I would--

00:10:16.681 --> 00:10:18.917
I would run that number now,

00:10:19.151 --> 00:10:22.421
which nobody asked me for
until months later.

00:10:24.857 --> 00:10:26.291
But it would have been--

00:10:26.391 --> 00:10:27.793
You know,
this is in the middle of COVID,

00:10:27.859 --> 00:10:29.561
and you're asking all these
people to do accounting.

00:10:29.661 --> 00:10:31.163
But, if that's a number

00:10:31.229 --> 00:10:33.065
that people would have
wound up wanting,

00:10:33.198 --> 00:10:35.734
which they didn't say
for months, Bill,

00:10:36.101 --> 00:10:39.738
I would have put that number
upfront in the beginning.

00:10:39.838 --> 00:10:41.540
People died
in a lot of nursing homes

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:43.408
simply because
the people who take care

00:10:43.475 --> 00:10:45.444
of the people
in the nursing homes are poor,

00:10:46.778 --> 00:10:48.147
and the poor people get COVID.

00:10:48.247 --> 00:10:49.915
But-- And it goes back
to incentives.

00:10:49.982 --> 00:10:53.819
We have social media algorithms
that get more engagement

00:10:53.886 --> 00:10:56.221 line:0%
and more Nissan ads
if they call out people

00:10:56.488 --> 00:10:57.489 line:0%
and really make them look

00:10:57.556 --> 00:10:58.991 line:0%
as stupid
and as mean as possible.

00:10:59.091 --> 00:11:00.993 line:0%
I was on the board
of my kids' school during COVID.

00:11:01.293 --> 00:11:03.262 line:0%
I wanted
a harsher lockdown policy,

00:11:03.329 --> 00:11:05.164 line:0%
and in retrospect,
I was wrong.

00:11:05.597 --> 00:11:09.301
The damage to kids of keeping
them out of school longer

00:11:09.534 --> 00:11:10.803
was greater than the risk.

00:11:10.936 --> 00:11:14.039
But here's the bottom line,
myself,

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:17.409
our great people at the CDC,
I'd like to think the governor,

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:20.412
we were all operating
with imperfect information,

00:11:20.479 --> 00:11:21.647
and we were doing our best, so--

00:11:21.713 --> 00:11:26.318
-It's-- It's-- Well...
-(APPLAUSE)

00:11:26.418 --> 00:11:29.555
-So let's-- let's learn from it.
-BILL: But--

00:11:30.155 --> 00:11:32.324
Let's learn from it.
Let's learn from it.

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:33.959
Let's hold each other
accountable,

00:11:34.192 --> 00:11:36.495
but let's bring a little bit
of grace and forgiveness

00:11:36.595 --> 00:11:38.464
in the shit show that was COVID.

00:11:38.564 --> 00:11:40.199
-(APPLAUSE)
-ANDREW: Yes.

00:11:40.299 --> 00:11:41.767
But other states
did do it differently.

00:11:42.234 --> 00:11:43.102
SCOTT GALLOWAY: Yeah.

00:11:43.435 --> 00:11:47.706
I mean, I read Florida asked
for two negative COVID tests

00:11:47.773 --> 00:11:49.441
before you could get back
into the nursing home.

00:11:49.874 --> 00:11:51.710
The-- Look, if you want to--

00:11:51.777 --> 00:11:53.645
First, on Scott's point,
he's exactly right.

00:11:53.778 --> 00:11:56.849
All the early information
was wrong, as a matter of fact.

00:11:57.015 --> 00:11:58.984
-BILL: A lot of it now is.
-Not just from President Trump,

00:11:59.217 --> 00:12:01.753
Dr. Fauci,
who I have great respect for,

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:04.256
early on said this
is not going to be a problem.

00:12:04.756 --> 00:12:05.657 line:0%
They said it was--

00:12:05.757 --> 00:12:09.361 line:0%
You could contract it
from touching a surface.

00:12:09.494 --> 00:12:10.830 line:0%
We sprayed all the subways.

00:12:10.896 --> 00:12:11.830 line:0%
BILL:
I remember washing the mail.

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:13.666
-And we had--
-(LAUGHTER)

00:12:13.732 --> 00:12:15.700
Yes. I still do, by the way.

00:12:15.767 --> 00:12:17.670
(LAUGHTER)

00:12:17.736 --> 00:12:22.475
But we had no idea
how infectious it was

00:12:22.608 --> 00:12:25.910
for seniors versus children,
early on.

00:12:26.044 --> 00:12:26.946
We had no idea.

00:12:27.012 --> 00:12:28.280
We didn't know
if it was airborne,

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:29.548
if it was by touch.

00:12:30.149 --> 00:12:31.483
So, this is--

00:12:31.684 --> 00:12:34.052
And when I say
it's all hindsight now,

00:12:34.219 --> 00:12:36.455
now you know, and the way--
Scott's exactly right.

00:12:36.555 --> 00:12:38.257
You know how it got
into the nursing homes?

00:12:38.524 --> 00:12:41.594
It got in months before
we knew it was here.

00:12:42.061 --> 00:12:44.629
The staff walked it
into the nursing home.

00:12:44.696 --> 00:12:45.864
BILL: Like I said,
the poor people.

00:12:46.098 --> 00:12:47.700
-That's a lot of what it is.
-ANDREW: That's exactly
how it got in.

00:12:47.766 --> 00:12:50.769
And also, I mean, it's a war.
In war, people die.

00:12:50.903 --> 00:12:52.404
And generals...

00:12:52.972 --> 00:12:54.740
(CLICKS TONGUE) ...it's not like
they're trying to kill them.

00:12:55.441 --> 00:12:56.475
-But generals--
-MELISSA: No.

00:12:56.542 --> 00:12:58.010
Generals have to make decisions.

00:12:58.143 --> 00:13:00.145
Not all decisions
are the right decisions.

00:13:00.579 --> 00:13:01.814
-You don't know until after.
-MELISSA: No.

00:13:01.881 --> 00:13:04.182
And to that point, I mean,
when you ask the question,

00:13:04.249 --> 00:13:05.217
"Would you
do things differently?"

00:13:05.284 --> 00:13:06.919 line:0%
I would do
everything differently.

00:13:06.985 --> 00:13:08.387 line:0%
-Every-- Literally everything.
-BILL: (LAUGHING) I agree.

00:13:08.453 --> 00:13:09.588 line:0%
There's nothing
I wouldn't do differently.

00:13:09.655 --> 00:13:10.956 line:0%
BILL: I never understand
these people in interviews

00:13:11.023 --> 00:13:12.691 line:0%
when they go, "Do you have
any regrets in life?"

00:13:12.758 --> 00:13:15.093
-"No, I don't. No--" Like, what?
-(LAUGHTER)

00:13:15.193 --> 00:13:17.329
-And that's why we have
to tell the story...
-Who-- Right.

00:13:17.396 --> 00:13:18.363
...and we have to understand it,

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:19.932
because there will
be another pandemic,

00:13:20.032 --> 00:13:21.266
and we've gotta learn
from this one,

00:13:21.333 --> 00:13:22.701
so we do it better
the next time.

00:13:22.801 --> 00:13:25.837
-All right, this is...
-(APPLAUSE)

00:13:25.904 --> 00:13:27.639
We'll make this the last one.

00:13:28.073 --> 00:13:32.110
This is for our
two New Yorkers here.

00:13:32.511 --> 00:13:35.214
"What do you New Yorkers think
of how Mayor Eric Adams

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:36.548
and Governor Kathy..."

00:13:36.682 --> 00:13:38.950
-"Hochul"? Uh, oh.
-(SIGHS) Yes.

00:13:39.017 --> 00:13:42.121
-Yes, you pronounced it right.
-(LAUGHTER)

00:13:42.187 --> 00:13:43.822
I take it you're a big fan.

00:13:43.922 --> 00:13:46.859
"Have handled
the city's migrant crisis?"

00:13:47.393 --> 00:13:48.560
Uh, I saw today,

00:13:48.627 --> 00:13:50.662
they're offering
any of these migrants,

00:13:50.829 --> 00:13:52.464
this sounds like a game show,

00:13:52.764 --> 00:13:55.667
a plane trip
to anywhere in the world.

00:13:55.734 --> 00:13:57.702
(LAUGHTER)

00:13:57.936 --> 00:13:59.871
This policy,
you don't have to go home,

00:13:59.971 --> 00:14:01.973
but you can't stay here.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.710
You know, Ed Koch
started that in the '80s

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:07.412
to help solve the homeless
crisis in New York City.

00:14:07.479 --> 00:14:09.114
He was giving out
one-way bus tickets

00:14:09.247 --> 00:14:11.650
to the homeless population.
This is not new, but yes.

00:14:11.717 --> 00:14:13.352
"See California,"
is what they said.

00:14:13.418 --> 00:14:14.986
-(LAUGHTER)
-MELISSA: Yes. They did.

00:14:15.053 --> 00:14:18.189
-Really, they did. It's true.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:14:18.256 --> 00:14:20.792
So, what do you think about how
they handled the migrant crisis?

00:14:20.892 --> 00:14:22.494
I-- You know, I just
wanna say something

00:14:22.561 --> 00:14:23.896
in defense of Mayor Adams.

00:14:24.463 --> 00:14:26.665
Immigration,
this migrant crisis,

00:14:26.732 --> 00:14:28.099
is by definition

00:14:28.233 --> 00:14:29.834
-a federal crisis.
-BILL: It is.

00:14:29.934 --> 00:14:31.670
This happened
because of decisions made

00:14:31.736 --> 00:14:32.971
by the Biden administration,

00:14:33.037 --> 00:14:34.706
and they basically
made them unilaterally,

00:14:34.773 --> 00:14:36.275
and then they didn't manage
any of it.

00:14:36.475 --> 00:14:38.176
And so,
people started showing up.

00:14:38.276 --> 00:14:40.845
The governor's not stepping in,
the president's not stepping in.

00:14:40.912 --> 00:14:42.814
And so Eric Adams
is doing everything he can

00:14:42.914 --> 00:14:45.718
to try to manage
this influx of people which--

00:14:45.818 --> 00:14:48.287
the likes of which we haven't
seen since World War Two

00:14:48.387 --> 00:14:49.321
-in New York City.
-BILL: Right.

00:14:49.387 --> 00:14:50.421
He doesn't have
the money to do it.

00:14:50.488 --> 00:14:53.091
He doesn't have
the human resources to do it.

00:14:53.191 --> 00:14:54.560
-BILL: Or the space.
-The space.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:55.794
New York's crowded
to begin with.

00:14:55.861 --> 00:14:57.429
Yeah. And then it's like
we have to turn around

00:14:57.496 --> 00:14:58.496
and say thank you,

00:14:58.563 --> 00:15:01.400
because the president's
offering to let us lease

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.535
Floyd Bennett Field,
where we can put people.

00:15:03.602 --> 00:15:05.804
It's like, what?
You caused this problem,

00:15:05.871 --> 00:15:07.139
and we're supposed
to say thank you

00:15:07.239 --> 00:15:10.175
'cause you're freeing up a field
for us to build migrant beds?

00:15:10.275 --> 00:15:11.810
It's just the craziest thing
in the world.

00:15:11.877 --> 00:15:14.880
So, I mean, my heart goes out
to Eric Adams,

00:15:14.947 --> 00:15:16.882
because he really was put
in a terrible position,

00:15:16.949 --> 00:15:18.049
and nobody else is stepping up.

00:15:18.116 --> 00:15:20.719
(APPLAUSE)

00:15:20.819 --> 00:15:23.989
But didn't some of these places
sort of deserve it?

00:15:24.055 --> 00:15:26.792
I'll ask you this, by saying,
"We're a sanctuary city."

00:15:26.892 --> 00:15:30.796
Like, how these terrible
racist border states were worse.

00:15:30.862 --> 00:15:32.464
And then,
when they called their bluff,

00:15:32.598 --> 00:15:34.967
and sent the migrants to them,
they were like suddenly,

00:15:35.067 --> 00:15:36.601
they weren't quite so liberal.

00:15:36.735 --> 00:15:39.037
They're what I call
"liberal-in-theory" people.

00:15:39.204 --> 00:15:40.705
A lot of people
are liberal in theory,

00:15:40.772 --> 00:15:42.307
and when the migrants arrived,
they were like,

00:15:42.374 --> 00:15:43.575
"Oh, what the fuck?"

00:15:43.709 --> 00:15:48.446
-(LAUGHTER, APPLAUSE)
-Listen...

00:15:48.513 --> 00:15:50.149
the sanctuary--

00:15:51.382 --> 00:15:53.952
"Sanctuary city"
was used during Trump

00:15:54.052 --> 00:15:55.954
when we were talking
about illegals.

00:15:56.688 --> 00:15:58.457 line:0%
New York City said
it was a sanctuary city.

00:15:58.524 --> 00:16:00.359 line:0%
New York State said
it was a sanctuary state.

00:16:00.425 --> 00:16:01.760 line:0%
Many other cities did also.

00:16:01.927 --> 00:16:03.494 line:0%
This is nothing
to do with sanctuary.

00:16:03.828 --> 00:16:06.864 line:0%
These people are here legally.
That's what we want to forget.

00:16:07.131 --> 00:16:08.600
They're asylum seekers,

00:16:08.667 --> 00:16:11.136
they are here legally
under the law.

00:16:11.436 --> 00:16:13.371
-So, it's not about sanctuary.
-Well, they--

00:16:13.471 --> 00:16:17.376
But, for the federal government
to abrogate its responsibility,

00:16:17.776 --> 00:16:20.512
where we get to a point
where the Texas governor

00:16:20.579 --> 00:16:22.914
is really deciding
where migrants go.

00:16:22.981 --> 00:16:24.082
-Right.
-We'll send this to LA,

00:16:24.149 --> 00:16:25.117
we'll send this.

00:16:25.217 --> 00:16:27.986
New York City winds up
with 130,000,

00:16:28.086 --> 00:16:31.189
and there's no federal oversight
or management.

00:16:31.623 --> 00:16:34.192
And you give it to the one level
of local government

00:16:34.259 --> 00:16:36.895
that is least able
to deal with it,

00:16:37.062 --> 00:16:39.064
which is a city administration.

00:16:39.832 --> 00:16:41.734
And then the state says,
you know,

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:43.168
"You're on your own."
It's crazy.

00:16:43.302 --> 00:16:45.337
That's why I'm for
Dean Phillips for president.

00:16:45.504 --> 00:16:46.939
(LAUGHTER, APPLAUSE)

00:16:47.072 --> 00:16:49.541
All right, thank you, everybody.
Appreciate it.

